WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.865
- That would be like Walmart
opening up operations

00:00:02.865 --> 00:00:04.014
in North Korea.

00:00:04.014 --> 00:00:07.770
There are situations where being bad

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.064
is high advantageous.

00:00:09.064 --> 00:00:12.450
When you put forth effort, you get reward.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:16.769
You should be constantly revising
your conclusions, I think.

00:00:16.769 --> 00:00:18.958
You have to very consciously
differentiate yourself

00:00:18.958 --> 00:00:22.984
from where you think your
professional peer group is going.

00:00:22.984 --> 00:00:24.372
He didn't want to work.

00:00:24.372 --> 00:00:25.689
He had bad habits.

00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:26.833
He had a bad attitude.

00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:29.679
The systems that surround
doggie healthcare

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:31.577
and human healthcare are
profoundly different.

00:00:31.577 --> 00:00:34.130
You need to have practiced,
to have apprenticed

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.820
for 10,000 hours before you get good.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.304
If you are a doctor,
you have to understand

00:00:39.304 --> 00:00:41.702
that you are in the relationship business.

00:00:41.702 --> 00:00:44.651
We're just going to be doing
this for the entire game.

00:00:44.651 --> 00:00:46.543
And you're going to be
exhausted at the end.

00:00:46.543 --> 00:00:49.066
- He's a Canadian journalist,
best-selling author,

00:00:49.066 --> 00:00:50.036
and speaker.

00:00:50.036 --> 00:00:53.839
He's been a staff writer for
the New Yorker since 1996.

00:00:53.839 --> 00:00:56.584
He's written five books,
all five of which have been

00:00:56.584 --> 00:00:58.425
on the New York Times Best Seller list.

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:01.020
He's Malcolm Gladwell, and
here are his top ten rules

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.020
for success.

00:01:05.317 --> 00:01:07.454
- It's not enough to have
a great idea and the focus

00:01:07.454 --> 00:01:10.072
and the conscientiousness
to see it to fruition,

00:01:10.072 --> 00:01:14.772
you must have the strength and
the resolve and the courage

00:01:14.772 --> 00:01:17.281
to pursue that idea, even
when the rest of the world

00:01:17.281 --> 00:01:19.031
thinks you're insane.

00:01:19.927 --> 00:01:22.853
Time and time again, if
you look at the stories

00:01:22.853 --> 00:01:26.051
of extraordinarily
important entrepreneurs,

00:01:26.051 --> 00:01:30.788
there is almost always a moment
when they are the only ones

00:01:30.788 --> 00:01:33.393
who believe in the value
of what they're doing.

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:34.968
I tell in my book the story of--

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:36.257
My book, David and Goliath--

00:01:36.257 --> 00:01:40.104
the story of Ingvar Kamprad,
the guy who founds IKEA,

00:01:40.104 --> 00:01:44.088
and the crucial moment
in the story of IKEA

00:01:44.088 --> 00:01:46.149
is when he faces a boycott

00:01:46.149 --> 00:01:48.615
from the other furniture
manufacturers in Sweden.

00:01:48.615 --> 00:01:53.010
He's about to go out of
business, and in desperation,

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.621
he moves his operations
across the Baltic Sea

00:01:55.621 --> 00:01:59.063
from Sweden to Poland and
sets up shop in Poland.

00:01:59.063 --> 00:02:00.805
And that's what IKEA is.

00:02:00.805 --> 00:02:04.612
IKEA is essentially furniture,
shipped flat, made in Poland.

00:02:04.612 --> 00:02:07.666
That's the original
elevator pitch for IKEA.

00:02:07.666 --> 00:02:11.138
What's interesting about that
is that he does it in 1961.

00:02:11.138 --> 00:02:14.626
At the height of the Cold War,
at a time when East and West,

00:02:14.626 --> 00:02:16.803
when Communist world and
free world are closer

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:19.833
to outright war than at
any other time in history,

00:02:19.833 --> 00:02:22.450
a guy living in the West, Sweden,

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.598
crosses the pond to
Poland, the Iron Curtain,

00:02:25.598 --> 00:02:26.828
and sets up shop.

00:02:26.828 --> 00:02:30.480
You cannot imagine what a
controversial move that was.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.899
That would be like
Walmart opening operations

00:02:33.899 --> 00:02:35.419
in North Korea.

00:02:35.419 --> 00:02:38.618
Literally, it's on that level
of kind of eyebrow-raising,

00:02:38.618 --> 00:02:42.345
you've got to be kidding me,
who is this guy, kind of thing.

00:02:42.345 --> 00:02:45.851
But he does it, and he
persists, and he turns his back

00:02:45.851 --> 00:02:46.885
on all those critics.

00:02:46.885 --> 00:02:50.832
Why? Because he is a
deeply disagreeable person.

00:02:50.832 --> 00:02:53.349
Didn't need people to agree with him.

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:55.317
And that's how he's able to build IKEA

00:02:55.317 --> 00:02:58.655
into this extraordinary,
runaway success story.

00:02:58.655 --> 00:03:01.036
That's very hard to do.

00:03:01.036 --> 00:03:04.599
As human beings, we are
hardwired to want the approval

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:05.984
of our peers.

00:03:05.984 --> 00:03:09.630
There are situations where being bad

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.547
is highly advantageous.

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:14.775
And if you read--

00:03:14.775 --> 00:03:15.791
I don't go into this in the book--

00:03:15.791 --> 00:03:18.460
but if you've read Innovator's Dilemma,

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.954
it's what Innovator's
Dilemma is all about, right?

00:03:20.954 --> 00:03:24.942
The disruptive outsider is
the one who is incapable

00:03:24.942 --> 00:03:27.337
of meeting the marketplace needs

00:03:27.337 --> 00:03:29.594
as the market is traditionally defined.

00:03:29.594 --> 00:03:31.508
They can't do it, right?

00:03:31.508 --> 00:03:34.158
So what do they do? They
try a completely new,

00:03:34.158 --> 00:03:37.693
half-assed approach which in
the beginning doesn't work,

00:03:37.693 --> 00:03:40.002
right, but that very
nature of trying something

00:03:40.002 --> 00:03:41.755
completely outside the mainstream,

00:03:41.755 --> 00:03:44.612
they end up upending the...

00:03:44.612 --> 00:03:48.194
Were they any good, they would
never be forced to do that.

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:49.889
So it's the same kind of principle.

00:03:49.889 --> 00:03:53.819
When you put forth effort, you get reward.

00:03:53.819 --> 00:03:57.256
When you throw your heart and
mind and soul into something,

00:03:57.256 --> 00:03:59.763
you get something back.

00:03:59.763 --> 00:04:02.625
You know, in my book, I call
that notion, that belief,

00:04:02.625 --> 00:04:06.300
that effort brings
reward, "meaningful work".

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.343
And when you look at the lives of people

00:04:08.343 --> 00:04:11.570
who are really successful, what
you see over and over again

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:14.147
is this idea of meaningful work

00:04:14.147 --> 00:04:17.392
is embedded in their consciousness.

00:04:17.392 --> 00:04:21.559
You know, I tell the story
in the book of The Beatles.

00:04:22.751 --> 00:04:23.584
Everybody knows about The Beatles.

00:04:23.584 --> 00:04:26.191
They come here in 1964,
and the British Invasion.

00:04:26.191 --> 00:04:28.642
The most interesting
thing about The Beatles

00:04:28.642 --> 00:04:31.178
is what happened to them
before they came to America.

00:04:31.178 --> 00:04:32.049
(coughs)

00:04:32.049 --> 00:04:35.973
In 1959, when they were just kids,

00:04:35.973 --> 00:04:39.505
they were invited to go
to Hamburg in Germany

00:04:39.505 --> 00:04:42.228
to be the house band at a strip club.

00:04:42.228 --> 00:04:45.900
And they went there, and they
stayed there for months on end

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:50.067
and seven days a week, they
played eight hour sets,

00:04:51.185 --> 00:04:54.719
night after night in this strip club.

00:04:54.719 --> 00:04:57.823
And over the course of that
extraordinary crucible,

00:04:57.823 --> 00:05:00.808
that experience of playing,
they taught themselves

00:05:00.808 --> 00:05:03.363
how to be a great band, right?

00:05:03.363 --> 00:05:06.562
In fact, we think now that
by the time The Beatles

00:05:06.562 --> 00:05:08.324
came to America, they had played together

00:05:08.324 --> 00:05:10.844
as a live band 1200 times.

00:05:10.844 --> 00:05:12.663
We could go to all of the clubs

00:05:12.663 --> 00:05:14.694
on Friday night in San Francisco,

00:05:14.694 --> 00:05:17.063
where all the promising
young bands are playing.

00:05:17.063 --> 00:05:19.229
I submit to you that you
would not find a single band

00:05:19.229 --> 00:05:22.645
that has played together
1200 times, right?

00:05:22.645 --> 00:05:23.766
Just doesn't happen.

00:05:23.766 --> 00:05:26.770
So what made The Beatles special?

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:30.135
What made them special
is that they were willing

00:05:30.135 --> 00:05:34.566
to play together 1200 times,
willing to play eight hour sets

00:05:34.566 --> 00:05:37.146
seven nights a week,
for months at a stretch.

00:05:37.146 --> 00:05:38.205
And why were they willing?

00:05:38.205 --> 00:05:41.775
Because they believed in the
notion of meaningful work.

00:05:41.775 --> 00:05:44.573
They had an opportunity to
throw their heart and mind

00:05:44.573 --> 00:05:48.329
into something, and get something back.

00:05:48.329 --> 00:05:50.644
And that made all the
difference in the world.

00:05:50.644 --> 00:05:52.317
When Dick Nisbett...

00:05:52.317 --> 00:05:55.456
He makes a turn in his
career as a psychologist

00:05:55.456 --> 00:05:56.867
where he goes from...

00:05:56.867 --> 00:05:58.818
Remember, he thinks the
fundamental attribution error

00:05:58.818 --> 00:06:00.868
in the beginning is fundamental,

00:06:00.868 --> 00:06:03.675
and then he realizes,
actually, no, it's cultural.

00:06:03.675 --> 00:06:05.175
And he puts out...

00:06:06.073 --> 00:06:08.134
And it doesn't refute the
fundamental attribution error,

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:09.910
it deepens our understanding of it to say,

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:12.893
oh, actually, it has
roots in Western culture,

00:06:12.893 --> 00:06:14.174
but you see a very different...

00:06:14.174 --> 00:06:16.827
And actually, then he writes
that book about East/West,

00:06:16.827 --> 00:06:18.243
which is incredibly fascinating.

00:06:18.243 --> 00:06:19.976
So he gets two great books,

00:06:19.976 --> 00:06:21.476
out of going back,

00:06:23.326 --> 00:06:25.914
going over and correcting
his earlier position.

00:06:25.914 --> 00:06:27.247
That to me is...

00:06:28.652 --> 00:06:31.792
the model of how you ought to behave

00:06:31.792 --> 00:06:33.520
in the intellectual world.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.269
I think you should always double back,

00:06:35.269 --> 00:06:38.291
and say, now wait a minute,
this is more complicated here,

00:06:38.291 --> 00:06:39.965
we can kind of...

00:06:39.965 --> 00:06:44.435
And "contradiction" is too
strong a word, but you should be

00:06:44.435 --> 00:06:48.040
constantly revising your
conclusions, I think.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.319
My strategy has always been, you can't...

00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:53.402
you have to very consciously
differentiate yourself

00:06:53.402 --> 00:06:57.569
from where you think your
professional peer group is going.

00:06:59.273 --> 00:07:01.856
So to the extent that people...

00:07:03.528 --> 00:07:05.464
To the extent that
people migrate to things

00:07:05.464 --> 00:07:09.179
that are accessible online,
I feel I should migrate

00:07:09.179 --> 00:07:11.838
to things that are inaccessible online.

00:07:11.838 --> 00:07:13.521
So the value..

00:07:13.521 --> 00:07:16.286
Or to the extent that people
stop reading books and read,

00:07:16.286 --> 00:07:19.036
I feel I need to read more books.

00:07:20.694 --> 00:07:22.761
So what I've been trying
to do is to kind of...

00:07:22.761 --> 00:07:26.592
it's why I spend a lot of time
in actual, physical libraries

00:07:26.592 --> 00:07:28.929
reading things in hard copy,

00:07:28.929 --> 00:07:31.842
because there's a kind of

00:07:31.842 --> 00:07:34.789
a serendipity that you get when you...

00:07:34.789 --> 00:07:37.936
This is not in any way meant
as a criticism, by the way,

00:07:37.936 --> 00:07:41.550
of search engines, for example,
which are incredibly useful,

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:44.830
but they also have limitations.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.053
They reward a certain kind of serendipity,

00:07:47.053 --> 00:07:50.183
and they punish another
kind of serendipity, right?

00:07:50.183 --> 00:07:51.404
And if you really want to...

00:07:51.404 --> 00:07:53.727
If you're interested in
serendipitous learning as I am--

00:07:53.727 --> 00:07:56.960
Much of what I uncover is
uncovered serendipitiously--

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.979
you have to be a student of
all of the different mechanisms

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:04.204
of chance encounters with the unusual

00:08:04.204 --> 00:08:06.333
and the insightful.

00:08:06.333 --> 00:08:09.706
And so that means, not only
do I spend a lot of time

00:08:09.706 --> 00:08:12.004
screwing around online on databases,

00:08:12.004 --> 00:08:15.111
but I also very, very
consciously make sure

00:08:15.111 --> 00:08:16.880
that I go to physical libraries

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.849
and walk through the stacks.

00:08:20.849 --> 00:08:23.231
Even something as simple as
you're interested in one book,

00:08:23.231 --> 00:08:25.097
and you go and you just
look at all of the books

00:08:25.097 --> 00:08:29.264
that surround it, and the
connections are not always...

00:08:30.362 --> 00:08:32.871
There are connections between them,

00:08:32.871 --> 00:08:34.925
but it's a different kind of connection

00:08:34.925 --> 00:08:36.543
than they would be connected online.

00:08:36.543 --> 00:08:38.327
It's not a keyword connection, right?

00:08:38.327 --> 00:08:40.352
It's a thematic connection, or it's a...

00:08:40.352 --> 00:08:41.665
So there's all these sorts of...

00:08:41.665 --> 00:08:45.415
You have to be a student
of these kinds of...

00:08:47.176 --> 00:08:50.926
Of the different ways
in which ideas cluster.

00:08:51.845 --> 00:08:55.058
And so, I've thought a lot
about that in recent years,

00:08:55.058 --> 00:08:58.840
as a way of distinguishing
myself from other journalists.

00:08:58.840 --> 00:08:59.757
I remain...

00:09:01.581 --> 00:09:03.413
I'm uninterested in that topic.

00:09:03.413 --> 00:09:05.597
- [Interviewer] Which one,
the relationship between gift

00:09:05.597 --> 00:09:06.774
and practice?

00:09:06.774 --> 00:09:09.587
- No, I'm uninterested in natural gifts.

00:09:09.587 --> 00:09:12.360
I know they exist, and I know
there's such a thing as talent

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.193
- Right, right.
- And blah, blah, blah,

00:09:13.193 --> 00:09:16.286
I just feel like, and so what, right?

00:09:16.286 --> 00:09:20.072
Because there are people, I
mean, you're a basketball fan,

00:09:20.072 --> 00:09:21.238
I always think of Derrick Coleman.

00:09:21.238 --> 00:09:22.346
- Yes.

00:09:22.346 --> 00:09:24.708
- I remember chatting with a
guy that worked for the 6ers,

00:09:24.708 --> 00:09:26.430
and he said, you have to understand,

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:29.594
that Derrick Coleman was
the most gifted man ever

00:09:29.594 --> 00:09:31.687
to set foot on a basketball court.

00:09:31.687 --> 00:09:35.583
He said, you've heard
a million other names,

00:09:35.583 --> 00:09:36.997
nobody was as good as Derrick.

00:09:36.997 --> 00:09:39.586
And who has heard today, who thinks,

00:09:39.586 --> 00:09:41.500
who puts Derrick Coleman
up there on the pantheon?

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:43.574
No one. He didn't want to work.

00:09:43.574 --> 00:09:44.886
He had bad habits.

00:09:44.886 --> 00:09:45.983
He had a bad attitude.

00:09:45.983 --> 00:09:47.388
I mean, so what?

00:09:47.388 --> 00:09:50.511
Give me somebody who want to
go to practice in the morning,

00:09:50.511 --> 00:09:52.856
and who wants to try in games,
and I want to celebrate him.

00:09:52.856 --> 00:09:56.043
- If someone were to follow
you from the inception to,

00:09:56.043 --> 00:09:58.978
sort of picking a story
or identifying a study,

00:09:58.978 --> 00:10:02.525
to the full book, what
happens along the way?

00:10:02.525 --> 00:10:04.099
- Well, I don't really know.

00:10:04.099 --> 00:10:06.432
I mean, sometimes I start...

00:10:09.446 --> 00:10:12.135
There are about five or six times a year

00:10:12.135 --> 00:10:14.248
I go to the NYU library

00:10:14.248 --> 00:10:16.415
and I spend a couple days.

00:10:18.764 --> 00:10:21.445
Just browsing is too mild a term.

00:10:21.445 --> 00:10:24.873
But, wandering around, going
through millions of journals

00:10:24.873 --> 00:10:27.584
in the most serendipitous way I can.

00:10:27.584 --> 00:10:29.126
Just to kind of see what's out there,

00:10:29.126 --> 00:10:30.649
and see if I can stumble on something.

00:10:30.649 --> 00:10:32.925
- Without a clear goal or direction?

00:10:32.925 --> 00:10:34.678
- No goal whatsoever. No goal whatsoever.

00:10:34.678 --> 00:10:37.522
So there's that I do, as a regular basis,

00:10:37.522 --> 00:10:38.439
and then...

00:10:40.732 --> 00:10:41.928
I do a fair amount of speaking,

00:10:41.928 --> 00:10:44.509
and I always try and have
conversations with people

00:10:44.509 --> 00:10:47.077
well outside my world.

00:10:47.077 --> 00:10:50.653
So today, I gave a talk this
morning in Philadelphia,

00:10:50.653 --> 00:10:51.649
and I was talking to a guy.

00:10:51.649 --> 00:10:55.684
One of the guys there runs
a medical devices company.

00:10:55.684 --> 00:10:57.866
Very, very small one.

00:10:57.866 --> 00:10:59.724
And so I started talking to him about it.

00:10:59.724 --> 00:11:00.882
Because I've always had
this idea in my head

00:11:00.882 --> 00:11:03.360
that it would be really,
really fun to write,

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:07.527
to compare the way dogs are treated

00:11:08.889 --> 00:11:10.096
with the way humans are treated,

00:11:10.096 --> 00:11:13.096
because they're not that dissimilar,

00:11:13.933 --> 00:11:16.759
as problems for medical science.

00:11:16.759 --> 00:11:20.727
But the systems that surround

00:11:20.727 --> 00:11:21.842
doggie healthcare

00:11:21.842 --> 00:11:24.387
and human healthcare are
profoundly different.

00:11:24.387 --> 00:11:28.367
So, the same devices

00:11:28.367 --> 00:11:31.239
are used in various hip implants,

00:11:31.239 --> 00:11:33.677
well, not the same, but analogous devices,

00:11:33.677 --> 00:11:36.869
only you do a complex
knee surgery on your dog,

00:11:36.869 --> 00:11:38.001
it's $7,000,

00:11:38.001 --> 00:11:39.934
and you do it on a human
being, it's a hundred.

00:11:39.934 --> 00:11:44.167
Now, is human really 15 times more complex

00:11:44.167 --> 00:11:45.880
than a dog, when it comes to...?

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:46.798
No, so there's something...

00:11:46.798 --> 00:11:49.066
But I sort of had this vague thought

00:11:49.066 --> 00:11:50.449
and when I met this guy,

00:11:50.449 --> 00:11:51.449
so I started asking about this,

00:11:51.449 --> 00:11:54.096
and he sort of started riffing
on it, gave me his card.

00:11:54.096 --> 00:11:56.151
Now that's sort of how it
works, you know what I mean?

00:11:56.151 --> 00:11:58.937
You take advantage of a little
thought you had in your head

00:11:58.937 --> 00:12:00.991
and when you meet someone
by accident who happens

00:12:00.991 --> 00:12:02.746
to have specialized knowledge,

00:12:02.746 --> 00:12:04.794
you make sure you get his card.

00:12:04.794 --> 00:12:08.712
So a group of really brilliant
psychologists in the field

00:12:08.712 --> 00:12:12.101
of expertise research have
sat down to try and figure out

00:12:12.101 --> 00:12:14.953
how long do you have to work at something

00:12:14.953 --> 00:12:16.936
before you become really good.

00:12:16.936 --> 00:12:18.709
And the answer seems to be,

00:12:18.709 --> 00:12:20.503
it's an extraordinarily consistent answer

00:12:20.503 --> 00:12:22.303
in an incredible number of fields,

00:12:22.303 --> 00:12:25.350
and that is, you need to have
practiced, to have apprenticed

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:28.222
for 10,000 hours before you get good.

00:12:28.222 --> 00:12:31.956
So, every great classical
composer, without exception,

00:12:31.956 --> 00:12:33.442
composes for at least 10 years

00:12:33.442 --> 00:12:34.875
before they write their master work.

00:12:34.875 --> 00:12:38.057
- Mozart...
- Mozart is composing at 11,

00:12:38.057 --> 00:12:40.268
but he's composing garbage at 11.

00:12:40.268 --> 00:12:43.613
He doesn't produce something
great until he's 22 or 23.

00:12:43.613 --> 00:12:45.897
Concerto number nine, I think. (mumbles)

00:12:45.897 --> 00:12:48.783
If I asked you, how long did
it take you when you were

00:12:48.783 --> 00:12:50.484
doing this job before
you felt comfortable,

00:12:50.484 --> 00:12:51.317
and good at what you were doing.

00:12:51.317 --> 00:12:53.223
- Yeah, 10 years at least
- 10 years.

00:12:53.223 --> 00:12:54.056
And the same with me.

00:12:54.056 --> 00:12:55.236
It's an incredibly consistent finding,

00:12:55.236 --> 00:12:56.463
and it's really important

00:12:56.463 --> 00:13:00.590
because it says that we are
far too impatient with people.

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:03.812
When we assess when someone
has got what it takes

00:13:03.812 --> 00:13:06.499
to do a certain job, we always
want to make that assessment

00:13:06.499 --> 00:13:09.780
after six months, or a
year, and that's ridiculous.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.744
The kinds of jobs we have people do today

00:13:12.744 --> 00:13:15.829
are sufficiently complex
that they require a long time

00:13:15.829 --> 00:13:17.013
to reach mastery.

00:13:17.013 --> 00:13:18.114
And what we should be doing

00:13:18.114 --> 00:13:21.078
is setting up institutions and
structures that allow people

00:13:21.078 --> 00:13:23.965
to spend the time and
effort to reach mastery,

00:13:23.965 --> 00:13:25.166
not judging them prematurely.

00:13:25.166 --> 00:13:27.972
If you are a doctor,
you have to understand

00:13:27.972 --> 00:13:31.031
that you are in the relationship business.

00:13:31.031 --> 00:13:33.870
If you are in a shipper,
you have to understand

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.621
that what you are interested
in is the supply chain

00:13:36.621 --> 00:13:40.788
from A to B, not in managing
some transaction along the way.

00:13:41.641 --> 00:13:44.164
If you're in the radio
business, you have to understand

00:13:44.164 --> 00:13:46.445
that you are not selling a box,

00:13:46.445 --> 00:13:49.539
you are selling an experience.

00:13:49.539 --> 00:13:53.266
Those are the rules of the modern economy.

00:13:53.266 --> 00:13:57.578
And those who grasp those
rules are the ones who thrive

00:13:57.578 --> 00:14:01.683
and succeed, and are capable
of transforming the industries

00:14:01.683 --> 00:14:02.972
that they're a apart of.

00:14:02.972 --> 00:14:06.127
The choice you have as an underdog is,

00:14:06.127 --> 00:14:08.794
if you're going to choose to fight,

00:14:09.782 --> 00:14:11.701
and fight to win,

00:14:11.701 --> 00:14:13.888
there are a series of
strategies available to you.

00:14:13.888 --> 00:14:16.709
But they are all more costly
than the strategies available

00:14:16.709 --> 00:14:18.641
to the favorite.

00:14:18.641 --> 00:14:22.391
So I give the example
of a chapter about a...

00:14:23.335 --> 00:14:24.809
a software mogul

00:14:24.809 --> 00:14:26.334
in Silicon Valley, an Indian guy,

00:14:26.334 --> 00:14:30.618
who coaches his 12-year-old
daughter's basketball team.

00:14:30.618 --> 00:14:33.377
And they are without talent.

00:14:33.377 --> 00:14:37.294
They are, by his own admission...

00:14:38.542 --> 00:14:40.295
They don't even know how to play the game.

00:14:40.295 --> 00:14:43.017
He takes them all the way to
the national championships.

00:14:43.017 --> 00:14:44.968
He does it by...

00:14:44.968 --> 00:14:47.347
He instructs them, they're going
to play the full-court press

00:14:47.347 --> 00:14:49.524
every minute of every game,

00:14:49.524 --> 00:14:52.564
and defend every inch of the court.

00:14:52.564 --> 00:14:56.153
Now, that's actually a
very effective strategy,

00:14:56.153 --> 00:14:59.153
particularly in age class basketball

00:15:00.183 --> 00:15:01.826
if you're the underdog.

00:15:01.826 --> 00:15:03.075
It's really your only chance of winning,

00:15:03.075 --> 00:15:04.754
is to play a really aggressive defense.

00:15:04.754 --> 00:15:08.515
It requires, however,
that everyone on your team

00:15:08.515 --> 00:15:12.896
expend maximum effort
every minute of the game.

00:15:12.896 --> 00:15:15.811
You cannot loaf for an instant.

00:15:15.811 --> 00:15:17.472
You have to be in really good shape,

00:15:17.472 --> 00:15:19.144
and you have to run yourself ragged,

00:15:19.144 --> 00:15:20.850
and you cannot let up.

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:22.558
Most people will not play that way

00:15:22.558 --> 00:15:25.283
because it's too difficult.

00:15:25.283 --> 00:15:26.727
If I said to you, we're
not going to be doing

00:15:26.727 --> 00:15:31.497
elegantly designed plays and
shooting gorgeous baskets

00:15:31.497 --> 00:15:35.372
and passing the ball, we're
just going to be doing this

00:15:35.372 --> 00:15:37.170
for the entire game, and
you're going to be exhausted

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:41.251
at the end, most kids would
say, that's not why I signed up.

00:15:41.251 --> 00:15:45.168
So that's a classic
illustration that effort...

00:15:46.554 --> 00:15:47.767
Effort is the route,

00:15:47.767 --> 00:15:50.110
one of the routes
available to the underdog.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:51.610
I can outwork you.

00:15:52.524 --> 00:15:55.947
I may not be able to outspend
you, but I can outwork you.

00:15:55.947 --> 00:15:59.290
Well, outworking is a tall order,

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:00.957
it's not easy to do.

00:16:03.137 --> 00:16:06.491
As anyone who's worked in a startup knows,

00:16:06.491 --> 00:16:09.365
it's one of the stressful parts of it.

00:16:09.365 --> 00:16:11.466
But successful startups...

00:16:11.466 --> 00:16:14.018
I've never seen any actual data on this...

00:16:14.018 --> 00:16:17.965
But do we think the people
working for successful startups

00:16:17.965 --> 00:16:19.422
work longer hours than people who work

00:16:19.422 --> 00:16:21.773
for Fortune 500 companies?

00:16:21.773 --> 00:16:23.219
My guess is yes.

00:16:23.219 --> 00:16:24.511
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:16:24.511 --> 00:16:27.439
I made this video because
Jim Jhang asked me to.

00:16:27.439 --> 00:16:29.436
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:16:29.436 --> 00:16:31.865
to profile next, leave
it in the comments below

00:16:31.865 --> 00:16:32.813
and I'll see what I can do.

00:16:32.813 --> 00:16:35.273
I'd also love to know
which of Malcolm Gladwell's

00:16:35.273 --> 00:16:38.612
top 10 rules meant the most to
you, had the biggest impact.

00:16:38.612 --> 00:16:40.794
Leave it in the comments, and
I'll join in the discussion.

00:16:40.794 --> 00:16:42.141
Thank you so much for watching.

00:16:42.141 --> 00:16:45.724
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

